    {
      "title": "Rewrite both terms as powers of x",
      "body": "<p>The two terms are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Integrate these separately.</p>",
      "recap": "Roots and reciprocal powers use the same integration power rule as positive integer powers.",
      "pitfall": "The denominator <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> belongs only to the second term, not to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Increase each exponent by 1 and write down the two new exponents.",
      "recall": [
